00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.390
that I was invited to do
at the Wiesner Gallery.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.550
Thank you so much, Sarah,
for all your support

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:49.715
and to all the staff members
who made this possible.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:56.090
So I'm just going to give a
very brief description of what

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:02.360
the work is, because there was
already a gallery walk through,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.920
which will be made
available shortly.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.199
And also, the link to the show
will be put in the description

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:10.699
at some point, which you're
more than welcome to go and take

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:12.080
a look at it.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.620
But so my work was--

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.810
the specific project,
Botanical Ghosts,

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.300
was part of a
three-year investigation

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.470
into the agricultural histories
and botanical histories

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:28.340
of Honduras, specifically in
Lencetilla Botanical Gardens,

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.190
which is located
in the north coast.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:36.500
And my art practice is a
multidisciplinary offering

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.470
for botanical imaginaries.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.240
And my thought of
this practice is

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.760
as a metabolism through which
internal becomings can happen.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.930
And today, I have the honor
of moderating this talk

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:54.030
between Semine and Shireen.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.730
I'm very excited
for the conversation

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:58.500
that will take place today.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.780
So first up, I would
like to introduce Semine.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.180
Semine Long-Callesen
is a historian

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.430
who recently received her
master's in architecture

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.080
studies in the history,
theory, and criticism of art

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.900
and architecture department
from MIT in 2020.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.780
Semine's work examines
colonial national museums

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.490
in Denmark, Malaysia,
and Singapore,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.160
as well as contemporary
artistic practices that

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.430
respond to such institutions.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.900
She holds a BA in art
history with distinction from

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.370
the University of Cambridge,
and she was also a [? Fialkov ?]

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:35.150
fellow and a [? Paulson ?]
researcher at MIT,

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.970
and is currently a fellow at
the Currier Museum of Art in New

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.900
Hampshire, as well as a
researcher at the Mexico

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.940
City-based architecture
practice, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.910
Her research has been
published in the Journal

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:50.980
of the Malaysian Branch
of the [? Roja ?] Asiatic

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:55.030
Society and the Danish National
Gallery's Perspective Journal.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.040
And today, Semine
will share with us

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.980
both part of her research and
her recipe website, Garden

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.830
Blues, which is a personal
iteration of the work

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.680
that she's done in the archive.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.050
And Shireen Hamza is a historian
and a current PhD candidate

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.750
in history of science
at Harvard University.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.029
Her dissertation project
is a reassessment

00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:17.950
of the concept of
Islamic medicine

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.649
by studying scholarly
medical traditions

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:23.340
across the Indian Ocean world.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.670
Working on texts of both Galenic
and Ayurvedic medicine composed

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.100
in Arabic and Persia between--

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.580
in Arabic and Persian between
the 13th and 15th centuries,

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.550
she shows the coexistence
of, and exchange between,

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.250
multiple traditions
of healing, as well as

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.350
the transformation
of disease categories

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.680
and medicinal substances
in Yemen and Gujrat.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.200
Her work has been supported
by the Social Science Research

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.420
Council and the
Fulbright Commission,

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.670
and she's the managing editor
for The Ottoman History

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.040
Podcast, and wrote and
produced Ventricles,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.110
a podcast on science
outside of the West.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:02.530
So I would like to just talk a
little bit about the thoughts

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:08.170
behind organizing this talk
and why Semine and Shireen--

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.230
I had the privilege of being
in conversation with them

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:16.120
and this coming
together really fluidly.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.930
And the dialogue between us
spans several geographies

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:23.320
and temporalities, and
this [INAUDIBLE] thing

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.720
of botanical exchanges,
sensory encounters

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.570
and archival histories
lie the practices

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.820
of Shireen and Semine,
whose work I deeply admire

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:32.350
and have been inspired by.

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:37.510
The title of this talk
comes from an essay

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.720
published by Prudence
Gibson on the color green.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.740
And I wanted to read to you
a very small excerpt where

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:46.270
she writes.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.300
"It is due to the loss of green
from our everyday habitats

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.850
that greenness is more
than a hue of nostalgia.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.200
It has also become a keen
political weapon, an activated

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.950
and dynamic arena of
civic and civil debate.

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:03.220
Thinking green is the same
as thinking for the long now.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.130
Our plans and concepts need
to have longevity and long

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.400
distance strategic clout."

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:14.380
So to me, this phrase, this
title, The Loss of Green,

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:19.120
invokes the presence of
an ecological past, what

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.220
Anna Tsing and Donna
Haraway referred

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:24.730
to as the plantation
[INAUDIBLE],,

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:28.090
which has been ongoing
through negotiations

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:32.220
of what is considered to
be living and non-living.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.170
In some ways, my
desire for this talk

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.390
was to look at this
ecological present

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.460
and speculate on the
future through the lens

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.560
of this vegetal past.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.840
What does the now look
like after centuries

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.360
of hybridizations,
of bioprospecting,

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.270
and of the ongoing extractive
relationships between humans

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.390
and the vegetal world?

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:52.230
Both Semine and
Shireen's practices

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.220
unearthed knowledge through
different sensoriums,

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:56.910
from the sense of
taste and smell

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.400
and the various
cuisines that make

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.340
their appearance in Semine's
project, Garden Blues,

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:03.920
to Shireen's engagement
with descriptions of plants

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:07.010
and medicinal practices
through the written word.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:10.040
Beyond solely images,
both of their work

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.230
take on the role of
animating the specimens

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:14.790
and manuscripts in the archive.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.710
What drew me to their
respective practices

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.890
was how they engage with the
affect of these repositories,

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.120
challenging and blurring
the roles of the historian

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.710
and the witness.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:28.550
So please welcome both
Semine and Shireen.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:32.680
I am going to hand it over
to our first presenter, which

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:37.670
I believe is Semine,
if I'm not mistaken.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:41.170
So I'm just going to hand
it over to you, and welcome.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:48.080
Hi, everyone, and thank
you for joining us tonight.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.050
It's so good to
see so many friends

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:55.230
and familiar faces,
and new ones, too.

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:57.000
Nancy, thank you
for the introduction

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.590
and for inviting me to be here.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.000
I'm excited to talk
with you and Shireen

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.620
and share my thoughts with you
guys and with everyone else.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:12.480
So as Nancy mentioned,
my work focuses mostly

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:16.905
on British colonial history and
colonial history in Malaysia,

00:08:16.905 --> 00:08:20.700
or Malaya as it was known.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.420
And I've looked specifically
at colonial museums

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.670
during the 19th century.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:31.380
They existed in most places
in the British empire

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:37.770
and were large repositories of
mineral and plant specimens.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:43.380
And the museums were important
to colonial administration,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:46.710
because they functioned
as laboratories

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:51.590
that worked in the service of
a general search for revenue.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.160
So in Malaysia, these
museums carried out

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.800
botanical experiments
with rubber and palm oil,

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.880
and eventually, these cash
crops transformed the landscape

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.260
of Malaysia and turned it into
a very lucrative colony of one

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:12.760
of the main exporters of
rubber and oil, palm oil.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.830
So curiously, later
in the 20th century,

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:22.230
museums came to play a role
of guardians of nature.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:26.190
They spearheaded conservation,
nature conservation

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.820
as nature was disappearing
outside the museum

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.230
walls due to the industries
that the museums themselves

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:37.570
had engineered.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:39.190
So the museum has
this double role

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.590
of both destroying
and preserving.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.270
And the colonial museum
also became a mirror of self

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:51.880
for specific parts of
the independence movement

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.250
in Malaysia.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:57.370
So they were initially part of
a big colonial infrastructure

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:01.000
that rendered Malaya
readable and exploitable

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.980
and governmental.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:06.010
But these collected
artifacts and specimens

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:09.160
of natural history
were also adopted

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.590
by nationalist
movements, and they

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:16.170
came to represent a
distinct national identity.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:20.510
So thoughts about colonial
botany and industries

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:25.290
and identity has been
on my mind for a while.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.220
And when I met Nancy
a couple of years ago

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:32.960
and we started cooking
together, we're realizing that--

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:37.920
we realized that there were
a lot of overlapping flavors.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.360
And this was a surprise
to me, because in my head,

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.190
Honduras is very far
away from Malaysia.

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:49.190
But as we dug into the
history of these places,

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.650
we realized that
there were lots of--

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:59.000
the similarities were due to
the geographies being ports

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:02.210
in the same imperial networks.

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:05.030
So Honduras and Malaysia
are not formally or fully

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:07.010
part of the same
empires, but people

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:10.720
like Dorothy Popenoe
and Wilson Popenoe, who

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.980
are the protagonists,
so to speak,

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:18.080
in Nancy's "Botanical Ghosts,"
her artwork, these two figures

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.680
were American and British
explorers and discoverers

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.530
who traveled to
Malaysia and Honduras

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:29.000
and documented plant species
there and carried with them

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:32.600
seeds from one botanical
station to another

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:39.690
across the tropical belt.
So from my experiences

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:43.800
with cooking with Nancy and
from working in the archive

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:50.150
emerged a more poetic
iteration of my research work.

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:53.490
It's a recipe website
called Garden Blues,

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:58.250
which is inspired by Honduran
and Malaysian cuisine.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:01.520
And I'm going to share
with you my screen

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.875
so you can see the website.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:12.950
OK.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:22.440
So I should say that,
in creating this site,

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:27.660
I've received a lot of
help, especially from Agnes

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:31.230
Cameron, who built it
with me and who thought

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.370
with me about its story.

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:35.640
So big thank you to Agnes.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:40.350
So the landing site is a
bunch of gardens and rivers

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:45.330
that are named after memories
and themes and places.

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:50.580
And each garden and river
is populated by flowers

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:53.160
or wavy little squiggles.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.110
And if you click on a
flower, it will take you

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:59.560
to a recipe that's associated
with the theme of the place.

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:06.570
So if we go to the Taiping
Lake Gardens, which

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:14.930
is in the north of
Malaysia, a recipe

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:19.170
for this coconut pan
fried cake will come up.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:32.654
And if we go to a Garden
of Cash Crops down here,

00:13:32.654 --> 00:13:36.520
a recipe for the beloved
plantains will come up.

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:42.150
And then lastly, if we go
to the Garden of Blues,

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:45.060
another dessert will come up.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:49.650
So this site also has an
index with the list of recipes

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:54.990
and a writing blog where
I think about the theory

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:56.940
and the histories that
is behind this site.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:08.890
So through food, I
think this recipe site

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:14.110
tells a story about
itinerant colonial nature

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:17.050
that travel through the tropics.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:21.680
And it also tells the
story about hybridity.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:26.990
I think it's a pushback against
nationalist ideas of where

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:29.330
things belong.

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:33.470
It's a pushback
against authenticity,

00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:37.040
and personally,
it's also disturbing

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:42.230
the lines between what we
think of as local and foreign.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:45.530
And I think, in that way,
it's a reflection of what

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:48.140
it means to come
from a territory that

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:53.450
is much larger than the
countries that we grew up in.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.160
So that's it for me now.

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:57.980
I'm going to pass the
spotlight to Shireen

00:14:57.980 --> 00:14:59.480
and stop screen sharing.

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:01.484
Thank you.

00:15:01.484 --> 00:15:04.480
Thank you, Semine.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:05.820
Thank you so much, Semine.

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.900
That was so, so
interesting, and I

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:11.510
can't wait for the discussion.

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:15.340
So I'm a historian of Medicine
in the medieval Indian--

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:17.800
or sorry, the medieval
Islamic world,

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:22.300
a time before European empires
shaped, and were shaped by,

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:24.670
capital S science.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:27.430
And I spend my days reading
manuscripts written out

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:30.760
over the centuries by physicians
who lived in the regions

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.460
bordering the
Indian Ocean world.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:37.450
The manuscripts that
you see here all

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:41.260
feature descriptions of
plants in alphabetical order,

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:44.440
or rather, those
parts of plants that

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.570
were most relevant to medicine.

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:50.140
So physicians needed to know
the therapeutic application

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:52.280
of these parts.

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:56.620
But with the exception of
those few who actively foraged

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:59.470
and studied botany,
they did not encounter

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:03.070
plants growing in their
environments as wholes.

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:06.550
Foragers, farmers,
traders, they were

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:10.150
the ones who supplied
markets with the dried roots,

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:14.140
stems, leaves, flowers, resins,
powders that were actually

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:15.910
used by physicians.

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:17.860
And these processed
forms of the plant

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:20.710
could travel, whether
to local markets

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:24.040
or to those across the ocean.

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.350
While there was a more
rarefied tradition

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.780
of illustrated
botanical manuscripts,

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:34.040
even these centered human uses
of, and relations to, plants.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:36.640
They never claimed to
offer a universal system

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:38.500
of classification.

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:40.450
The descriptions
that you see here

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:43.630
included information on
where the plant grows,

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:47.080
its different variants, the
color of its usable parts

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:49.630
and their taste and
smell in relation

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:52.300
to other medicinal substances.

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:56.080
These were a practical
set of comparisons.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:59.080
But when I close my
eyes and try to picture

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:01.690
the history of botany,
I don't actually

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:05.530
imagine the millions of extant
pages of Arabic and Persian

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:09.220
manuscripts in libraries
across Eurasia and the US.

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:12.700
I see instead the bright
illustrated plant typologies

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:15.099
of European colonial
botany, which

00:17:15.099 --> 00:17:17.619
I have encountered as
framed prints hung up

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:21.819
on the walls of countless
inns, cafes, and shops.

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:24.010
The more history
I learn, the more

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:27.069
sinister my encounters with
these aestheticed forms

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:30.670
have felt, erasure
after erasure.

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:35.530
As historian, Daniela
Bleichmar, has argued,

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:39.490
efforts to make the empire
visible always made parts of it

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:41.590
invisible.

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:43.870
The authors were those
who could lay claim

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:45.970
to the intellectual
work of observing

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:49.990
and classifying, writing it
all down in colonial languages.

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:52.900
Unmentioned were those
doing the manual labor

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:57.250
of procuring the specimens or
of painting their portraits.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.530
Nancy's work in
"Botanical Ghosts"

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:02.950
brilliantly evokes the
macabre lives and afterlives

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:06.100
of these images, made
by people who, quote,

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:10.510
"chose to accept the
death of many things."

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:13.780
18th century naturalists
aspired to represent plants

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:18.010
as more real than reality,
a generalized typology,

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:21.460
accessible only with great
skill and difficulty.

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:24.490
They were intentionally
and aggressively selective,

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:26.830
extracting the typical
from the particulars

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:29.560
of individual
specimens, not, quote,

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:33.760
"distracted by irrelevant
details and accidental traits,

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:38.380
a leaf torn by an insect, a
wilting flower, or a flower

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:40.630
not fully bloomed."

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:43.160
Historians, Lorraine
Daston and Peter Galison

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:46.840
call this kind of
knowing truth to nature.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:50.320
Idealized botanical
images had a generality

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:53.260
that transcended the
species to, quote,

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.890
"reflect a never seen,
but nonetheless real,

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:58.990
plant archetype."

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:02.860
But as Bleichmar and Londa
Schiebinger have argued,

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:07.810
this kind of botanical
expertise was so highly valuable

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:11.200
in the 18th century,
where botany was, quote,

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:14.500
"big business and big science."

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:17.380
Botanical expeditions
were expensive,

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:20.440
deploying large teams
of naturalists, artists,

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:24.070
and laborers, and requiring
the active participation

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:27.550
of a whole imperial
administrative apparatus,

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:30.820
governors, treasury officials,
physicians, pharmacists,

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:34.420
clergymen and many
other local populations,

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:38.200
town to town in the Americas.

00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:41.830
Botanical images were implicated
in their investors' desires

00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:43.600
for future profit.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:47.380
Economic botany shaped the
fate of uncountable plants

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:52.000
and people who were commodified
within a plantation economy.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:54.700
The violent ends to which
these images contributed

00:19:54.700 --> 00:19:58.030
are another major difference
from the medieval traditions

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.290
of botany and
pharmacopeia that I study.

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:05.790
Nancy transforms idealized
illustrations of the ackee tree

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:09.720
into a uniformly gray
rotating 3D model,

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:11.940
gesturing at the
continuity of truth

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:15.780
to nature in contemporary
cultures of scientific modeling

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.200
and de-familiarizing these
ubiquitous and highly

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.720
aestheticized illustrations.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:24.510
In the narrative of "Botanical
Ghosts," I witnessed

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:28.890
Dorothy's senses heighten,
her boundaries of self

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:34.140
dissolve into the specificity of
Lencetilla's lives and deaths.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:38.070
In imagining this intensely
local story in Dorothy's lives,

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:45.250
the sense of "thereness"
that Nancy describes, and--

00:20:45.250 --> 00:20:45.750
sorry.

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:48.930
In imagining this intensely
local story in Dorothy's life

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:51.750
and the sense of
"thereness" that she felt,

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:56.010
Nancy evokes a time scale
that transcends that of empire

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:59.700
and challenges the
totalizing universal way

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:04.980
of seeing and classifying that
the Linnaean system proposed.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:08.190
Truth to nature images
often divorced plants

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:10.290
from the environment
where they grow

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:12.600
and the culture and
knowledge of the people

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:15.240
who cultivate or
forage them, quote,

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:17.640
"rejecting the
local as contingent,

00:21:17.640 --> 00:21:21.120
subjective, and translatable,
favoring instead

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:23.670
the dislocated
global as objective,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.730
truthful, and permanent."

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:28.620
A lot changed from
the composition

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:32.310
of these medieval medical
texts in the 14th century,

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:36.360
to Carl Linnaeus and the 18th,
to Dorothy Popenoe in the 20th.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.720
But I was really surprised to
find a moment of recognition

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:42.390
in this sentence of the
narrative of "Botanical

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:43.860
Ghosts."

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:46.050
"Dorothy told
herself she was not

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:49.380
one to believe in spirits,
especially the ones

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:53.670
that she heard about among
the workers of Lencetilla."

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:56.340
Though learned medieval
sciences were not

00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:59.580
as totalizing as the modern
sciences have become,

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:02.280
nor as separate or
privileged within hierarchies

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:04.290
of knowledge,
their practitioners

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:07.620
still policed the boundaries
of their disciplines.

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:10.560
While farmers, miners,
traders, and foragers

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:14.310
had valuable knowledge
physicians and botanists relied

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:17.640
on to learn local
particulars and to locate

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:20.940
beneficial plant, animal,
and mineral substances,

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:24.660
their names only seldom
enter the texts as sources

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:27.240
and do not have the
same prestige as those

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:30.090
of ancient textual authorities.

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:33.150
Though, these relationships
were quite different from that

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:37.710
of Dorothy's to the
workers on Lencetilla

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:40.860
the continuity of
scientific exclusion

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:44.940
was one that I was
unprepared to face.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:48.840
Nancy does not dwell on this
racist and classist violence

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:50.400
in Dorothy's discourse.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:52.890
She mentions it
somewhat obliquely,

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.830
privileging instead the
voice of the ackee tree

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:59.040
and Dorothy's terrifying
and transformative encounter

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:00.150
with it.

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:04.080
At a recent Union Docs event,
artist, Madeleine Hunt-Ehrlich,

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:10.140
spoke about abstraction as a
form of opacity and opacity

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:12.540
as a strategy used
by Black artists

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:15.240
as a way to protect
themselves and their audiences

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.190
from historical violence.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:19.560
This struck me as an
apt way of understanding

00:23:19.560 --> 00:23:22.410
Nancy's engagement with the
colonial archive of botany

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:23.910
in Honduras.

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:26.190
She still makes
images to mediate

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:29.490
something of this
visual past, however

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:31.380
opaque the transmission.

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:33.870
But it is a history
thoroughly haunted

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:37.050
by the radical
vibrancy of plants.

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:39.030
Thanks.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:42.150
Thank you so much, Shireen
and Semine, both of you,

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:45.930
for these really amazing
and incredible presentations

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:48.240
of your work.

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:52.350
I'm very excited to start to
just jump into the discussion

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:56.310
so we have as much
time as we can.

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:57.870
But I really--

00:23:57.870 --> 00:23:59.240
I wanted to have--

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:02.400
maybe start out with a
question that both of you

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:08.940
could answer about your
experiences as historians.

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:15.470
And I would really like
to know a little bit more

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:20.270
about the sensory and
affective experiences

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:24.110
that you've both experienced
in these different archives

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:31.880
and repositories and the kind of
things that do not get written

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:36.890
into the academic texts and
these kind of projects that

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:41.420
are, I think, challenging
these boundaries again

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:42.860
of historian/witness.

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:46.720
And I would like to enter
the conversation there,

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:50.550
so I was wondering if
one of you could jump in.

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:54.260
And we could do just unmute and
get the conversation flowing.

00:24:59.955 --> 00:25:01.050
I can start.

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:08.550
Yeah, it's interesting how
behind the writing of history,

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:11.910
there is a lot of
archive work that

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:16.950
goes into producing text, which
is not necessarily included.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:23.760
I think something that I didn't
mention in my presentation,

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:27.240
and that also doesn't come
through in the recipe website,

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:33.360
is that in the natural
realm, included people.

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:38.550
And anthropological
studies of people

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:43.080
were also appearing
in studies of plants.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:51.280
So there is this curious--

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:55.990
the British natural history
includes both locals,

00:25:55.990 --> 00:26:00.490
different races and religion,
what today is categorized

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:02.230
as cultural artifacts.

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:06.670
But then, in the early colonial
museums in the 19th century,

00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:09.920
these were
categorized as nature.

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:12.640
And I was speaking
to this a little bit

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.470
in this idea of
the colonial museum

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:17.410
transitioning into
the national museum.

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:25.240
So the natural resource,
both labor and nature,

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:28.480
later on becomes
this mirror of self.

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:32.986
Like locals see
themself mirrored

00:26:32.986 --> 00:26:35.710
in natural history museums.

00:26:35.710 --> 00:26:39.010
The natural resource
becomes this--

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:40.720
enters the cultural
realm, it becomes

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:43.580
like a cultural resource.

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:45.730
Yeah.

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:46.510
Yeah.

00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:47.650
No, that's so interesting.

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:49.210
And, Semine, when
you were talking

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:54.175
about the porosity of
different empires and how,

00:26:54.175 --> 00:26:55.900
even though your
two family histories

00:26:55.900 --> 00:27:00.070
may be filed away into
two separate empires,

00:27:00.070 --> 00:27:02.470
I thought that was
really interesting how

00:27:02.470 --> 00:27:06.940
they come together, in
the kitchen, at least,

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:08.620
and how we can't
really just study

00:27:08.620 --> 00:27:12.730
Spanish empire or British
empire as two separate things.

00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:15.250
But I also loved
to hear what you

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:17.620
said about the relationship
between museums

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:21.280
and conservation and
the kind of temporality

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:26.140
of the past, the idea of
salvage that animated so much

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:27.100
of anthropology.

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:28.960
And I'd love to
hear, at some point,

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:34.930
you say more about what
role museums did play

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:36.970
in this idea of conservation.

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:42.430
But yeah, I wanted to
answer Nancy's question

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:48.460
by talking about where all those
manuscript images you guys saw,

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:50.290
where I encountered them.

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:56.470
And it was all in the UK
and online World Digital

00:27:56.470 --> 00:28:00.400
Library or Yale University's
Beinecke Library.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:02.800
These are all
places really diff--

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:06.730
far and removed from
the context where

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:08.350
these manuscripts were written.

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:13.030
So when I go to, say, the
British Library's Oriental

00:28:13.030 --> 00:28:19.030
Studies reading room, which is
full of these portraits of, I

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:24.430
don't know, major people
in British colonial history

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:30.370
from Asia and Africa, it
feels so strange to me.

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:34.720
There was a moment when I was
there during my dissertation

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:40.900
research where there's a
manuscript of a 14th century

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:44.410
pharmacological and medical
text [INAUDIBLE],, which

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:45.940
is a Persian text.

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:48.280
And the British
Library has a copy

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:52.120
of this text copied
by the author's son.

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:54.730
So it's really close
to the author's life

00:28:54.730 --> 00:28:58.960
and this very kind
of a precious object.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:03.700
And I don't know how many
people ever really order

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:05.930
it or want to see it.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:09.077
But if it was in a
different context,

00:29:09.077 --> 00:29:10.660
I think there would
be a lot of people

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:13.180
who would be really happy
to be able to access

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:17.620
that manuscript somewhere,
maybe where Persian is spoken.

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:21.490
And so those things
happen every day.

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:25.188
And it's so strange to be--

00:29:25.188 --> 00:29:27.730
to know-- to recognize that the
knowledge system I'm studying

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:28.700
is hierarchical--

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:30.820
I'm not trying to idealize it.

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:33.445
But to just be--

00:29:33.445 --> 00:29:35.050
to look at in the
face every day,

00:29:35.050 --> 00:29:38.950
that this is removed
from its context,

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:42.670
either by theft or by
purchase under duress,

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:46.540
or any number of ways that
these manuscripts make

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:49.180
their ways into these
libraries and archives,

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:53.110
and then to balance that with
the moments of discovery,

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:56.500
moments of, oh this is
so great for my research,

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:59.780
to feel happy while
handling these manuscripts,

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:04.090
it feels very like
competing moments.

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:06.580
And that's why I
really connected

00:30:06.580 --> 00:30:11.020
with that aspect of your
project, Nancy, this

00:30:11.020 --> 00:30:14.290
entering an archive of
violence but finding

00:30:14.290 --> 00:30:18.580
very human narratives and
constructing and speculating

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:21.400
as to the subjectivity
of those people,

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:24.160
rather than just
shutting it out.

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:25.135
Right.

00:30:25.135 --> 00:30:26.510
Yeah, thank you
so much, Shireen.

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:29.080
And I mean, I think
that something else

00:30:29.080 --> 00:30:34.540
that I thought that both
of your works were doing

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:42.105
was also challenging very
much the ways in which

00:30:42.105 --> 00:30:45.670
the construction of
nature was happening

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:48.400
through different senses.

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:51.220
And I briefly mentioned
in the introduction,

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:55.930
and, Semine, yours, it's through
this exchange of flavors,

00:30:55.930 --> 00:30:58.480
and tastes, and smells,
and cuisines that

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:04.735
was happening, and,
Shireen, through these--

00:31:04.735 --> 00:31:09.730
the descriptions of plant
species and medicinal practices

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:14.830
that require a certain,
perhaps, other kind of knowledge

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:15.760
that is--

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:19.870
I don't know if it exists
any longer or doesn't.

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:25.660
But, Semine, I was curious
whether there was any way

00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:29.650
in which these readings or
expansions of the senses when

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:33.280
it comes to imagining
vegetal imaginaries,

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:35.350
what can we learn
today about that?

00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:38.530
Or what are those
things challenging

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:45.390
in terms of this very visual
history that centers vision

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:50.260
as the sole way of constructing
nature or imagining

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:52.150
and remembering?

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:55.965
And so I don't know, I was
curious to hear a little bit

00:31:55.965 --> 00:31:57.340
more about that
from both of you,

00:31:57.340 --> 00:32:04.100
because these things that
are engaging different parts,

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:10.960
different, essentially,
sensory encounters in history,

00:32:10.960 --> 00:32:14.700
I think, challenge
existing narratives.

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:17.460
So I was wondering if you could
both briefly talk about that.

00:32:23.622 --> 00:32:26.000
I don't know, Shireen, maybe.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:29.150
Well, I definitely-- I
mean, that was definitely

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:31.010
the point of the
Zoom background,

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:35.690
just to show that for so many
people using these plants,

00:32:35.690 --> 00:32:38.090
there are no illustrations
in front of them.

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:40.870
They're not encountering
the plant as a whole.

00:32:40.870 --> 00:32:43.350
They're reading and memorizing.

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:45.470
There's a lot of
memorization involved, so

00:32:45.470 --> 00:32:48.170
the textuality of the practice.

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:52.970
I didn't include too many
images of a poetic text,

00:32:52.970 --> 00:32:55.700
but a lot of the
texts I'm looking at

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:59.390
are in didactic poetry so
people can memorize them.

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:04.820
And the inseparability
of object and text,

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:09.860
whether that's memorized
and oral or being read aloud

00:33:09.860 --> 00:33:12.680
from a text, is really present.

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:15.350
And those were
often comparisons.

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:20.180
They were often about, OK,
this powder of this root

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:22.850
will taste slightly more
bitter than this other thing

00:33:22.850 --> 00:33:25.280
that you will definitely
have tasted before.

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:31.160
And so it evokes an image of
these folks in the bazaar,

00:33:31.160 --> 00:33:37.010
in a marketplace, just tasting
stuff and ingesting medicines

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:41.645
to make sure that the quality
and the identification

00:33:41.645 --> 00:33:44.480
of the substance is correct
and it will actually

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:46.670
be therapeutic.

00:33:46.670 --> 00:33:50.060
And I would like
to say that this--

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:54.470
some forms of this medical
tradition are still practiced,

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:58.520
and there definitely are people
who will keep this alive,

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:01.520
whether it's recognized by
post-colonial nations like

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:03.440
India and Pakistan
or in Bangladesh,

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:05.900
where Unani medicine-- is
usually what it's called

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:06.680
today--

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:11.179
is a regulated by the
state, or whether it's,

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:13.520
for example, in the
Middle East or in parts

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:16.760
of like East Africa,
or even West Africa,

00:34:16.760 --> 00:34:18.500
this sort of tradition
is practiced.

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:23.570
But it's not recognized
to the same level

00:34:23.570 --> 00:34:25.040
in most of these countries.

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:28.730
It's biomedicine is
medicine, and everything else

00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:33.770
is alternative or
traditional and it does face,

00:34:33.770 --> 00:34:36.500
because of the lack of resources
and lack of regulation,

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:40.260
often people are
sometimes harmed by it.

00:34:40.260 --> 00:34:44.270
But people are harmed
by biomedicine, too.

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.400
So it's pretty
complicated, but I

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:48.110
would say that
there are definitely

00:34:48.110 --> 00:34:55.670
people who continue to interact
with medicines through senses

00:34:55.670 --> 00:35:00.710
like primarily smell and
taste more than sight.

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:04.100
But I also know that those who
practice this tradition today,

00:35:04.100 --> 00:35:06.980
their education is
completely different

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:10.160
and often has become
biomedicalized

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:14.450
and that the sight
has thus become more.

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:16.490
But yeah, I visited
some of these schools

00:35:16.490 --> 00:35:21.110
of Unani medicine, and
there's a laboratory of--

00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:23.540
one of the classrooms
that I went to

00:35:23.540 --> 00:35:28.400
had these jars of just
powders and dried roots.

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:31.280
And they had-- this is
like a wall of jars.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:33.170
And the students who
graduate from that

00:35:33.170 --> 00:35:36.050
need to be able to
identify each of those

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:38.870
without knowing
what the label says.

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:42.050
So there definitely are
people who still are training

00:35:42.050 --> 00:35:43.970
in these ways of knowing.

00:35:43.970 --> 00:35:44.810
Yeah.

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:47.360
I find that actually
also a really good segue

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:50.270
into a question that I have
for Semine's work, which

00:35:50.270 --> 00:35:53.480
had to do with this relationship
that you briefly mentioned,

00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:57.050
Semine, between the museum,
the garden, and the laboratory,

00:35:57.050 --> 00:36:01.520
which there seems to be this
continuity of transformation

00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:04.430
that happens between--

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:08.270
in these places that began
as experimental botanical

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:13.890
stations, or places for,
essentially, biolo--

00:36:13.890 --> 00:36:18.680
essentially, a kind of
laboratory in a [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:21.475
would then become
these other things.

00:36:21.475 --> 00:36:23.600
And I would love to hear
you talk a little bit more

00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:27.190
about that and how it
comes about in your work.

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:29.200
Sure.

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:33.030
So the museum
buildings would often

00:36:33.030 --> 00:36:37.800
have attached to them a
smaller botanical garden where

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:40.170
the people who
worked at the museums

00:36:40.170 --> 00:36:46.050
experimented with seeds
and different plants.

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:49.680
And I think what we
have talked about is

00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:54.000
that these botanical
institutions

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:57.450
in the colonial context
were deeply involved

00:36:57.450 --> 00:37:01.425
with extraction and displacement
of people and importing labor.

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:09.780
But they were also strange
places where amateurs worked.

00:37:09.780 --> 00:37:13.380
The British colonial offices
were not necessarily trained

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:18.330
or educated in biology or
botany, so a lot of them

00:37:18.330 --> 00:37:24.960
were steered by eccentric people
who had botany as a hobby,

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:28.710
and a lot of the collections
were accidentally assembled.

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:35.820
So these collections do-- can
come across as encyclopedic.

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:39.330
And Shireen, you
mentioned taxonomy.

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:44.760
They can have this very
overwhelming and powerful

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:45.660
impact on you.

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:49.080
But they were also often
chaotic bureaucracies

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:51.210
with small budgets.

00:37:51.210 --> 00:37:54.745
And when they were
then passed on to--

00:37:54.745 --> 00:37:56.370
thinking about the
transformation, when

00:37:56.370 --> 00:38:02.350
they were passed on
to national movements,

00:38:02.350 --> 00:38:09.775
they became sort of a nostalgic
place for what has once been.

00:38:09.775 --> 00:38:17.390
They were sites of abundance, of
a lost diversity, biodiversity.

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:20.560
So yeah, I mean,
they are strange.

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:23.080
The botanical gardens,
the ones in Honduras,

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:24.490
and the ones in
Malaysia as well,

00:38:24.490 --> 00:38:27.160
are strange places,
because they supposedly

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:31.720
reflect a reality outside of
their little delineated space.

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:35.680
But those are usually
quite extraordinary places

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:39.510
with a very high diversity.

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:41.140
Yeah.

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:44.940
Yeah, and I think,
I mean, speaking--

00:38:44.940 --> 00:38:51.660
in terms of these dilineations,
I cannot help to think about

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:58.000
these kind of plantation logics
that would then emerge from

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:03.070
these really specific
science of experimentation.

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:09.292
And [INAUDIBLE] particularly
that experimental station,

00:39:09.292 --> 00:39:15.750
it basically produced a matrix
of living things, which--

00:39:18.430 --> 00:39:22.660
plantation logic will
attempt to create hierarchies

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:28.510
of organisms, of life that are
embedded into these logics,

00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:30.940
but they have a
history that comes

00:39:30.940 --> 00:39:36.100
from all of these relationships
that have been constructed

00:39:36.100 --> 00:39:40.960
throughout history
that are passed down

00:39:40.960 --> 00:39:44.200
through these collections.

00:39:44.200 --> 00:39:47.230
There's almost an
assembly line that

00:39:47.230 --> 00:39:50.440
produces these kind of
extractive relationalities.

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:54.820
And so I really
find that through--

00:39:54.820 --> 00:40:00.310
by looking at these histories
and these collections,

00:40:00.310 --> 00:40:05.140
small collections, one is able
to almost see into the present

00:40:05.140 --> 00:40:08.410
and perhaps extrapolate
into the future.

00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:11.170
And-- which brings me
to another question

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:12.400
that I have to both of you.

00:40:15.430 --> 00:40:18.640
I guess I don't know
if this is maybe

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:20.543
too much of a
positive thing, but I

00:40:20.543 --> 00:40:22.210
was curious if you
have thoughts on what

00:40:22.210 --> 00:40:26.890
can be learned from readings
of the vegetal imagination,

00:40:26.890 --> 00:40:30.610
botanical history
for our present.

00:40:30.610 --> 00:40:37.740
And can these things help
us create other imaginaries

00:40:37.740 --> 00:40:42.840
and other relationships
to nature and to plants,

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:44.280
the vegetal world?

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:50.730
So yeah, I don't know if any of
you have any thoughts on that.

00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:54.810
I've honestly not been
thinking about this that long.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:59.310
I know there are people here
who are thoroughly plant people.

00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:02.460
And I don't think that I've
kept a plant alive for longer

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:06.360
than three or four years,
so they're still alive.

00:41:06.360 --> 00:41:08.970
But this is a relatively
recent chapter in my life,

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:11.430
so maybe I should
let others say.

00:41:11.430 --> 00:41:14.010
Like I want to plug the
work of Nicholas Roth, who's

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:18.630
on this call, who's doing an
amazing project about botany

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:21.600
in early modern South
Asia across Persia and in

00:41:21.600 --> 00:41:23.815
[INAUDIBLE],, and
he's a plant person.

00:41:23.815 --> 00:41:25.440
He sees like a
painting, and he's like,

00:41:25.440 --> 00:41:26.815
this this this
plant, and this is

00:41:26.815 --> 00:41:28.480
that plant, which is awesome.

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:30.360
But I have been
really influenced

00:41:30.360 --> 00:41:35.910
by Natasha Meyers' idea of
the planthroposcene and just

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:40.680
the idea that moving
somewhere different,

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:44.130
breaking the trajectory of
consumption in climate crisis

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:48.960
would really require tuning
into other ways of sensing

00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:50.190
and of being.

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:52.320
And it's something that
I think, Nancy, you

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:55.560
call microbial subjectivities
and vegetal life,

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:57.720
which I just love.

00:41:57.720 --> 00:41:58.590
Yeah.

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:02.290
But, Semine, I don't know if
you have thoughts on this.

00:42:02.290 --> 00:42:02.790
Yeah.

00:42:02.790 --> 00:42:05.220
I mean, Shireen, you--

00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:08.340
your work is about medicine
and, in a way, healing.

00:42:10.950 --> 00:42:17.010
And I want to return to the
idea of the botanical gardens

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:18.730
of the museums, the
colonial museums,

00:42:18.730 --> 00:42:20.730
as kind of contradictory spaces.

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:28.200
The certain botanical
stations, at least

00:42:28.200 --> 00:42:30.450
that I'm familiar
with in Malaysia,

00:42:30.450 --> 00:42:34.050
served, for instance,
as retreat places

00:42:34.050 --> 00:42:39.030
for Victorian English
women who had been

00:42:39.030 --> 00:42:45.010
diagnosed with frail health.

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:50.250
So I think that is a curious
doubleness to the gardens.

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:53.645
I also think it's--

00:42:53.645 --> 00:42:56.250
I mean, through my
little personal food

00:42:56.250 --> 00:43:00.630
projects and Garden
Blues, it's thinking

00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:04.620
about healing through
these places that

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:05.760
have done so much harm.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:10.570
I don't know if that
is actually possible.

00:43:10.570 --> 00:43:14.430
But I do think that there's
a lot of value in deepening

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:16.830
our knowledge
about these places,

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:19.260
and it gives you a
broader perspective

00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:20.550
of how we ended up here.

00:43:25.660 --> 00:43:27.010
Yeah.

00:43:27.010 --> 00:43:27.940
Oh, go ahead.

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:35.830
No, I was going to ask Shireen
about hybridity and plant

00:43:35.830 --> 00:43:40.970
exchanges, perhaps before
the colonial encounter,

00:43:40.970 --> 00:43:44.260
and if you can speak to
that a little bit more.

00:43:44.260 --> 00:43:44.800
Sure.

00:43:44.800 --> 00:43:46.540
Yeah, I've been
really influenced

00:43:46.540 --> 00:43:52.720
by Projit Mukharji's concept of
braided knowledge for Ayurveda,

00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:54.550
really thinking about--

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:56.500
I mean, he's working
on the colonial period,

00:43:56.500 --> 00:44:00.850
but he's pointing out that
all of the so-called "great

00:44:00.850 --> 00:44:06.040
traditions" were already
hybrid and plural,

00:44:06.040 --> 00:44:09.190
even when they started
to be encountered

00:44:09.190 --> 00:44:13.180
by Europeans more frequently
from the 16th century onward.

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:17.710
And so definitely, it has
been fascinating for me

00:44:17.710 --> 00:44:21.550
to be studying Ayurvedic
ideas in Persian

00:44:21.550 --> 00:44:27.010
and the selectiveness also of
which things are translated

00:44:27.010 --> 00:44:29.290
and which things are not.

00:44:29.290 --> 00:44:34.210
So I think there's a different
feeling about it, because--

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:40.090
about those kinds of braidings
because their science

00:44:40.090 --> 00:44:42.610
was not totalizing.

00:44:42.610 --> 00:44:46.960
It was not implicated so deeply
in how the state governs,

00:44:46.960 --> 00:44:50.860
and the state did
not have as invasive

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:53.320
a presence in the lives
of most individuals

00:44:53.320 --> 00:44:54.670
as the modern state.

00:44:54.670 --> 00:44:57.130
And so I feel like the
stakes of the question

00:44:57.130 --> 00:45:02.920
are quite different than
in, say, the 19th century.

00:45:02.920 --> 00:45:05.470
But yeah, I do really--

00:45:05.470 --> 00:45:08.320
even though hybridity
has gotten a lot of flak

00:45:08.320 --> 00:45:12.070
from a lot of people
for good reasons,

00:45:12.070 --> 00:45:14.500
I think it's really important
to think about traditions

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:16.000
as always already hybrid.

00:45:19.590 --> 00:45:20.090
Yeah.

00:45:20.090 --> 00:45:21.440
Thank you so much.

00:45:21.440 --> 00:45:23.750
So we actually just got
a really great question

00:45:23.750 --> 00:45:25.820
from Caroline Jones.

00:45:25.820 --> 00:45:27.684
Hi, Caroline.

00:45:27.684 --> 00:45:28.620
[LAUGHS]

00:45:28.620 --> 00:45:30.200
Thank you so much for coming.

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:34.700
So it's, she asks, "I'm
curious about the gender

00:45:34.700 --> 00:45:37.790
threads in the discussion
and Western early modernism.

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:41.660
Witches were the females in
command of botany and cures.

00:45:41.660 --> 00:45:43.340
The professionalization
of curing

00:45:43.340 --> 00:45:46.810
was a gender control
project among other things.

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:48.500
How does Nancy and
Semine and Shireen

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:50.795
see this question in the
non-Western context?"

00:45:53.690 --> 00:45:55.830
And yeah, that is a
really great question.

00:45:55.830 --> 00:46:00.568
I don't know if either
of you want to go first.

00:46:00.568 --> 00:46:01.610
I definitely [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:01.610 --> 00:46:04.310
I mean, I kind of want
to aim at you, Nancy,

00:46:04.310 --> 00:46:08.870
because you've got a
complicated intersection here

00:46:08.870 --> 00:46:10.040
where the colonialist--

00:46:10.040 --> 00:46:10.820
Yeah.

00:46:10.820 --> 00:46:12.150
--is female.

00:46:12.150 --> 00:46:12.650
Yeah.

00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:14.370
Right?

00:46:14.370 --> 00:46:20.100
So you know, I know that
Schiebinger's work is about--

00:46:20.100 --> 00:46:21.510
as well as Natalie Davis--

00:46:21.510 --> 00:46:27.720
about someone like [INAUDIBLE]
going on the ships of discovery

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:30.470
and then talking to the women
about the abortifacients.

00:46:30.470 --> 00:46:31.080
Yeah.

00:46:31.080 --> 00:46:32.220
Right?

00:46:32.220 --> 00:46:35.940
So there's a possibility
of intersectionality there

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:37.500
that's interesting.

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:41.160
But in your different
contexts, right, it

00:46:41.160 --> 00:46:43.410
might just be canceled out.

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:45.540
And for Shireen,
I'm also inspired

00:46:45.540 --> 00:46:50.505
by the work of the HTC
PhD student, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:46:50.505 --> 00:46:51.600
Maybe you know her.

00:46:51.600 --> 00:46:54.690
Anyway, she's working on poison
manuals, which are always

00:46:54.690 --> 00:47:00.080
written by men about
women as the ones who

00:47:00.080 --> 00:47:06.430
have to hold the potion or to
put some of their urine in it.

00:47:06.430 --> 00:47:09.320
It's very wet wrapped
through the female world

00:47:09.320 --> 00:47:11.960
of these Islamic medieval
texts she's looking at,

00:47:11.960 --> 00:47:12.920
these poison manuals.

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:17.090
But the women are not
doing the writing.

00:47:17.090 --> 00:47:19.320
So anyway, it's just
a grab bag question,

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:21.560
but I'd love to know
what you all think

00:47:21.560 --> 00:47:25.080
about these flows of power.

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:27.000
Yeah, absolutely.

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:29.910
I would like to address
the question of Dorothy,

00:47:29.910 --> 00:47:32.730
which is one that--

00:47:32.730 --> 00:47:36.210
Caroline, you were there at the
very beginning of this inquiry,

00:47:36.210 --> 00:47:41.010
and I will have to
admit that I don't think

00:47:41.010 --> 00:47:43.020
I have arrived to a
conclusion of what

00:47:43.020 --> 00:47:44.843
my relationship to this
character, Dorothy,

00:47:44.843 --> 00:47:45.510
specifically is.

00:47:45.510 --> 00:47:47.560
It's a very complicated one.

00:47:47.560 --> 00:47:50.580
It's an acknowledgment
that she, in her--

00:47:55.650 --> 00:48:00.360
she essentially helped
perpetrate and spread

00:48:00.360 --> 00:48:04.320
a very specific
kind of violence,

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:06.630
while at the same time
producing knowledge

00:48:06.630 --> 00:48:13.670
that was, at the time,
considered perhaps

00:48:13.670 --> 00:48:15.080
maybe forbidden or--

00:48:17.975 --> 00:48:23.210
I think that, in some
ways, the question

00:48:23.210 --> 00:48:30.755
of the control of gender
is really interesting,

00:48:30.755 --> 00:48:33.920
and it brings me, maybe,
to think about maybe

00:48:33.920 --> 00:48:37.590
more about the present,
because I think that there have

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:43.260
definitely been a sort of
revival of these practices

00:48:43.260 --> 00:48:50.990
that are perhaps considered
maybe pseudo-science.

00:48:50.990 --> 00:48:57.270
Or there's been this
revival of herbalism

00:48:57.270 --> 00:49:00.120
and, essentially, witchcraft.

00:49:00.120 --> 00:49:03.360
And I believe that
it has transcended,

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:05.430
in some ways,
gender, and we have

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:10.110
a new vision of
this relationship

00:49:10.110 --> 00:49:15.330
to magic and the occult, and
which is deeply, deeply related

00:49:15.330 --> 00:49:17.940
to these botanical
relationships, what

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:20.640
that might look like today.

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:25.300
And I don't know, I feel
that, Semine, I really

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:31.810
hear what you talked about with
this idea of healing and curing

00:49:31.810 --> 00:49:34.480
and that knowledge was--

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:36.340
this kind of botanical
knowledge was also,

00:49:36.340 --> 00:49:42.790
in some ways, gendered, or
this way of being in the garden

00:49:42.790 --> 00:49:44.800
was specifically also--

00:49:48.580 --> 00:49:49.675
yeah, very pointed.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:55.500
Yeah, sorry, I'm
getting all tongue tied.

00:49:55.500 --> 00:50:01.170
But yeah, Caroline, I feel
like it's complicated,

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:03.720
and I don't think I've arrived
to any kind of conclusion

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:07.410
or terminal point.

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:08.685
Good artistic position.

00:50:12.550 --> 00:50:15.660
Yeah, I mean, thank you so
much for this reference.

00:50:15.660 --> 00:50:17.662
I'm going to follow
up for the name

00:50:17.662 --> 00:50:18.870
of the scholar you mentioned.

00:50:18.870 --> 00:50:23.700
I'd also like to point to Sara
Verskin's book, Barren Women,

00:50:23.700 --> 00:50:26.670
which just came out last year.

00:50:26.670 --> 00:50:30.960
And it's really
interesting, because she

00:50:30.960 --> 00:50:35.940
is deeply interested in
women's knowledge and women's

00:50:35.940 --> 00:50:37.140
experiences.

00:50:37.140 --> 00:50:40.590
But her sources from the
medieval Islamic world

00:50:40.590 --> 00:50:41.910
are all written by men.

00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:44.730
And I believe she's
primarily working in Arabic.

00:50:44.730 --> 00:50:48.105
She also reads some
Hebrew sources as well.

00:50:48.105 --> 00:50:52.980
But what really comes through
from that book, something

00:50:52.980 --> 00:50:55.140
I'm really taking away
from it, is the intensity

00:50:55.140 --> 00:51:00.390
of male anxieties around women's
knowledge and women's spaces.

00:51:00.390 --> 00:51:05.020
And it's just astounding
sometimes to read,

00:51:05.020 --> 00:51:10.080
especially legal scholars,
not as much physicians,

00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:12.600
but intensely for
legal scholars,

00:51:12.600 --> 00:51:18.120
Islamic religious scholars,
ulema, how they are concerned.

00:51:18.120 --> 00:51:20.850
And they're not concerned
about witchcraft and magic

00:51:20.850 --> 00:51:21.600
explicitly.

00:51:21.600 --> 00:51:24.000
But what they are
concerned about

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:27.420
is that they have
the real Islam.

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:31.110
They have the proper and
textual Islam as male scholars.

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:33.630
And when women get
together, especially

00:51:33.630 --> 00:51:39.360
around intense moments of life
and transition like birth,

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:42.540
they don't know what kind of
practices they're transmitting,

00:51:42.540 --> 00:51:46.260
and they don't want that to
happen without somebody being

00:51:46.260 --> 00:51:51.490
there, some man being there
saying what the real Islam is.

00:51:51.490 --> 00:51:55.440
And so I think
that, unfortunately,

00:51:55.440 --> 00:51:59.070
as became clear to
me reading this book,

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:01.170
there aren't always
going to be questions

00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:05.970
that we can answer about what
women's spaces looked like

00:52:05.970 --> 00:52:08.670
and what their knowledge
looked like in this period,

00:52:08.670 --> 00:52:12.540
just because they were, except
in particular disciplines,

00:52:12.540 --> 00:52:15.820
like hadith
prophetic traditions,

00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:19.890
they were largely excluded
from textual production.

00:52:19.890 --> 00:52:23.250
So yeah, it's been hard to
grapple with that with reading

00:52:23.250 --> 00:52:25.820
that book this year.

00:52:25.820 --> 00:52:26.550
Yeah.

00:52:26.550 --> 00:52:31.140
I think Semine also had a
comment on this before we

00:52:31.140 --> 00:52:32.790
wrap up, as we're very close.

00:52:32.790 --> 00:52:34.630
But do you want
to go ahead and--

00:52:34.630 --> 00:52:35.130
Yeah.

00:52:35.130 --> 00:52:38.550
I can share my thought briefly.

00:52:38.550 --> 00:52:42.270
So I think it's a really
interesting question,

00:52:42.270 --> 00:52:46.110
because a lot of the colonial
officers and civil servants

00:52:46.110 --> 00:52:48.810
that I look at, and who
left behind the archive

00:52:48.810 --> 00:52:50.970
at the colonial
museums, were men.

00:52:50.970 --> 00:52:53.970
But the travelogues, there
are quite a few travelogues

00:52:53.970 --> 00:52:58.110
of Victorian women who traveled
across the British empire

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:03.570
and met all of
these civil servants

00:53:03.570 --> 00:53:06.060
and documented
their conversations

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:08.585
and described their
character and how

00:53:08.585 --> 00:53:10.710
they interacted with the
locals, their relationship

00:53:10.710 --> 00:53:12.330
with the locals.

00:53:12.330 --> 00:53:15.120
So in a way, this
travelogues for me

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:20.010
was this more like backstage
human relationship,

00:53:20.010 --> 00:53:24.300
what's going on between
the colonial officers.

00:53:24.300 --> 00:53:28.800
They were written by these
women who were very wealthy,

00:53:28.800 --> 00:53:32.370
and they spent their life
just traveling around.

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:35.140
But yeah, I think that's,
at least in the texts

00:53:35.140 --> 00:53:37.770
that I've encountered,
there's a different category

00:53:37.770 --> 00:53:40.800
of text that women
would have written,

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:43.230
a travelogue or
botanical illustrations.

00:53:43.230 --> 00:53:46.380
And those travelogues
is one of them

00:53:46.380 --> 00:53:50.723
that is specifically speaks
to the gender question, yeah,

00:53:50.723 --> 00:53:51.390
you are raising.

00:53:53.960 --> 00:53:56.537
Yeah.

00:53:56.537 --> 00:53:57.870
Yeah, and it's also interesting.

00:53:57.870 --> 00:54:01.470
I mean, a lot of botanical
illustrators were women.

00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:07.390
And it was-- for example, in
my project [INAUDIBLE] project,

00:54:07.390 --> 00:54:13.338
Dorothy Popenoe produced
a lot of the illustrations

00:54:13.338 --> 00:54:14.980
that Wilson Popenoe,
her husband,

00:54:14.980 --> 00:54:18.720
who was a really famous
botanist, published.

00:54:18.720 --> 00:54:30.110
And it was this labor of
reproducing this knowledge that

00:54:30.110 --> 00:54:34.690
was still almost
blanketed and obscured

00:54:34.690 --> 00:54:42.940
by the almost monumentalizing
of Wilson Popenoe's career.

00:54:42.940 --> 00:54:46.570
And so-- OK, Sarah just
gave me the little signal

00:54:46.570 --> 00:54:50.290
that we're very close
to wrapping up tonight.

00:54:50.290 --> 00:54:54.340
But if anyone would like to
stay afterwards and ask any more

00:54:54.340 --> 00:54:57.590
questions, you're
more than welcome to.

00:54:57.590 --> 00:55:01.630
But I wanted to say thank
you also to everyone who came

00:55:01.630 --> 00:55:04.090
and to Semine and Shireen.

00:55:04.090 --> 00:55:08.193
So if you would like to stay
and ask a couple more questions,

00:55:08.193 --> 00:55:09.360
you're more than welcome to.

00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:15.890
But if you have to
go, also no worries.

00:55:15.890 --> 00:55:21.950
But thank you so much, Sarah
and the Wiesner Gallery

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:24.800
for opening up this
opportunity and this space

00:55:24.800 --> 00:55:27.740
for having this really amazing
conversation with these two

00:55:27.740 --> 00:55:31.165
historians and also artists in
their own right whose work I

00:55:31.165 --> 00:55:32.460
deeply admire.

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:36.680
So thank you very much,
everyone, and yeah,

00:55:36.680 --> 00:55:40.020
hopefully see soon.

00:55:40.020 --> 00:55:42.020
Thank you so much to
our amazing panelists.

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:45.200
Yes, and those friends who
want to stay, you're welcome.

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:47.630
But really, we appreciate
everyone's participation.

00:55:47.630 --> 00:55:48.380
Thank you so much.

00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:49.314
Thank you.

00:55:54.930 --> 00:55:57.810
Hi, Nicholas.

00:55:57.810 --> 00:56:03.790
This is just sort of
bundle of questions.

00:56:07.798 --> 00:56:11.406
I'm not sure how
useful it is but--

00:56:11.406 --> 00:56:14.230
and how much is just
my own concerns.

00:56:14.230 --> 00:56:18.420
But one thing that I
was wondering about is,

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:23.670
as we were just talking about
colonial imperial contexts writ

00:56:23.670 --> 00:56:31.650
large, and as opposed to a
pre-colonial or non-colonial

00:56:31.650 --> 00:56:40.600
context with your work, Shireen,
I wonder to what degree you

00:56:40.600 --> 00:56:49.090
think that there are
peculiarities and whether they

00:56:49.090 --> 00:56:57.550
matter to botanical science
as implemented or as promoted

00:56:57.550 --> 00:57:04.250
by particular Western colonial,
quote, unquote, "cultures,"

00:57:04.250 --> 00:57:09.350
particular empires, especially
with the obsessive centrality

00:57:09.350 --> 00:57:13.250
to British imperial identity
that botany is for the Brits,

00:57:13.250 --> 00:57:16.610
to the point even
where they weren't--

00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:20.660
where British partners
are, and lady travelers who

00:57:20.660 --> 00:57:23.640
produce botanic illustrations
are all over the place,

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:27.232
even in colonies that
aren't historically British.

00:57:27.232 --> 00:57:27.732
Mm-hmm.

00:57:30.700 --> 00:57:35.570
And that-- and there's this
particular pattern that

00:57:35.570 --> 00:57:38.360
has been relatively well
studied and documented

00:57:38.360 --> 00:57:42.470
of gardens in particular
British colonies,

00:57:42.470 --> 00:57:46.100
like Malaya, like
India, and that end up

00:57:46.100 --> 00:57:49.670
having particular relationships
to the local elites

00:57:49.670 --> 00:57:55.990
and their production of
knowledge around this stuff,

00:57:55.990 --> 00:58:00.550
versus other European
empires, where

00:58:00.550 --> 00:58:04.150
I think we have a comparatively
more spottier record.

00:58:04.150 --> 00:58:07.740
I mean, Shireen mentioned
Daniela Bleeichmar's work

00:58:07.740 --> 00:58:13.750
on botany in the
Iberian colonial world.

00:58:13.750 --> 00:58:19.163
But there-- do those differences
and those dynamics matter?

00:58:19.163 --> 00:58:21.580
I was also just thinking that
with the-- we have so many--

00:58:21.580 --> 00:58:26.380
in a anglophone
slash Germanic world

00:58:26.380 --> 00:58:28.870
from early on, with
Marion and so on,

00:58:28.870 --> 00:58:30.670
we have all these
women producing

00:58:30.670 --> 00:58:32.740
botanical illustration.

00:58:32.740 --> 00:58:35.890
But for the Spanish
imperial expeditions,

00:58:35.890 --> 00:58:40.090
we have indigenous Mexican
artists, who are men

00:58:40.090 --> 00:58:43.450
but who are Mexican
born and Mestizo.

00:58:46.240 --> 00:58:48.250
Are there parallels
or connections there?

00:58:48.250 --> 00:58:50.800
I don't know, just bundle of
questions that might or might

00:58:50.800 --> 00:58:52.420
not be useful.

00:58:52.420 --> 00:58:54.960
Can I add one question,
Nicholas, since you're--

00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:55.460
Yes.

00:58:55.460 --> 00:58:55.960
--here?

00:58:55.960 --> 00:59:00.490
Which is, how do you think
about gender in botany

00:59:00.490 --> 00:59:02.810
in your work in early
modern South Asia?

00:59:06.090 --> 00:59:12.400
I haven't engaged with
it very thoroughly so far

00:59:12.400 --> 00:59:21.340
because the working of 16th
through 18th century South

00:59:21.340 --> 00:59:30.760
Asian sources, I have so few
female voices in the record.

00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:34.870
So where I've touched
upon it has merely mostly

00:59:34.870 --> 00:59:41.200
been in regard in the way
that horticultural spaces

00:59:41.200 --> 00:59:43.470
and horticultural
labor is imagined.

00:59:46.070 --> 00:59:50.740
And there, women feature, I
think, relatively prominently,

00:59:50.740 --> 00:59:53.890
but almost to the point of
it being a poetic conc--

00:59:53.890 --> 00:59:57.280
sort of a literary
conceit, I think.

00:59:57.280 --> 01:00:01.368
But this is always with
the caveat that these are

01:00:01.368 --> 01:00:06.970
the imaginings or
ideals of male authors,

01:00:06.970 --> 01:00:09.640
at least in the work that I've--

01:00:09.640 --> 01:00:13.750
materials I've looked
at so far and patterns

01:00:13.750 --> 01:00:14.950
that I've looked at so far.

01:00:20.110 --> 01:00:22.670
Thank you so much,
Nicholas, for that question.

01:00:22.670 --> 01:00:24.520
I'm an artist, and so
I feel like I'm not

01:00:24.520 --> 01:00:27.040
qualified to answer any
of that [INAUDIBLE]..

01:00:27.040 --> 01:00:32.740
But, Semine, I don't know if you
have an answer or a response,

01:00:32.740 --> 01:00:36.070
or if anyone would
also like to follow up

01:00:36.070 --> 01:00:38.380
with another question,
you're more than

01:00:38.380 --> 01:00:41.850
welcome to unmute
yourself and also ask one.

01:00:41.850 --> 01:00:44.872
I think what came to mind--

01:00:44.872 --> 01:00:46.830
I think this is more a
response than an answer.

01:00:50.730 --> 01:00:53.850
The botanical stations
and the hill stations

01:00:53.850 --> 01:00:59.700
that I'm familiar with were
both experimental gardens

01:00:59.700 --> 01:01:02.940
where British colonial
officers in Malaysia

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:06.600
collected species from across
the empire and also territories

01:01:06.600 --> 01:01:07.920
beyond.

01:01:07.920 --> 01:01:13.230
But they were at the same time
also little miniature versions

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:15.480
of British gardens.

01:01:15.480 --> 01:01:20.460
So I think this comes back to,
yeah, the contradictory spaces

01:01:20.460 --> 01:01:22.170
that these places are.

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:26.550
It's both a place of
foreign, quote, unquote,

01:01:26.550 --> 01:01:30.630
"foreign" imported
seeds and plants.

01:01:30.630 --> 01:01:35.760
But they were also
green spaces where

01:01:35.760 --> 01:01:37.650
I think the British
made themselves

01:01:37.650 --> 01:01:40.740
feel at ease or at home.

01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:45.180
Like rose gardens were
built next to experiments

01:01:45.180 --> 01:01:50.550
with tobacco and
silk and especially

01:01:50.550 --> 01:01:52.890
in the hills where
the climate was more

01:01:52.890 --> 01:01:54.795
suitable for European plants.

01:01:57.690 --> 01:02:03.390
Yeah, so there's the strange
coexistence in these gardens.

01:02:03.390 --> 01:02:10.080
Yeah, that's a comment I
think for you, Nicholas.

01:02:10.080 --> 01:02:10.650
Wow.

01:02:10.650 --> 01:02:13.110
That just reminds me of--

01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:15.510
I'm not super versed
in the scholarship,

01:02:15.510 --> 01:02:20.760
but the production of Persian
gardens all over South

01:02:20.760 --> 01:02:27.900
Asia and the post 14th century
explosion of these forms, yeah,

01:02:27.900 --> 01:02:32.370
we actually have an expert
here to talk about that.

01:02:32.370 --> 01:02:35.100
But yeah, I think that that--

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:37.980
I'm trying to grapple with the
part of Nick's question, which

01:02:37.980 --> 01:02:41.340
is like, what is
the distinction,

01:02:41.340 --> 01:02:45.630
say, for example, between the
expansion of Persian style

01:02:45.630 --> 01:02:48.780
gardens under the
sultanate into South Asia

01:02:48.780 --> 01:02:52.890
and the kind of British gardens
you're talking about, Semine.

01:02:52.890 --> 01:02:57.185
And I feel like it
really comes down

01:02:57.185 --> 01:03:00.230
to both labor and epistemology.

01:03:00.230 --> 01:03:02.640
It can't just be,
oh, the exportation

01:03:02.640 --> 01:03:04.860
of like ideas and aesthetics.

01:03:04.860 --> 01:03:09.960
It really also has to do
with the very unique kinds

01:03:09.960 --> 01:03:13.500
of racialized forms
of labor that emerge,

01:03:13.500 --> 01:03:15.780
I think, in European empire.

01:03:15.780 --> 01:03:18.690
And I just I don't think that--

01:03:18.690 --> 01:03:21.420
though there are certainly
social hierarchies

01:03:21.420 --> 01:03:24.540
in all parts of the
world, and caste

01:03:24.540 --> 01:03:30.150
deserves mention for sure for
South Asia, they're just not--

01:03:30.150 --> 01:03:32.820
I think yeah, that shifts
as well in the modern.

01:03:32.820 --> 01:03:36.510
And I feel like I
always come back

01:03:36.510 --> 01:03:38.520
to labor when I'm
trying to say what's

01:03:38.520 --> 01:03:43.170
the difference between premodern
and modern empire labor,

01:03:43.170 --> 01:03:49.440
and as well as just the totality
of scientific discourses.

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:52.410
Some of the-- like you
were saying, Nicholas,

01:03:52.410 --> 01:03:55.590
what do we do with these
idealized representations

01:03:55.590 --> 01:04:00.150
of women, for example, or of
specific groups or subgroups

01:04:00.150 --> 01:04:02.550
of people by elite writers?

01:04:02.550 --> 01:04:04.470
Well, those things
they were producing

01:04:04.470 --> 01:04:08.490
didn't actually
transform into policy.

01:04:08.490 --> 01:04:12.240
They didn't become
an imperial apparatus

01:04:12.240 --> 01:04:16.170
that then governed those
people's lives, at least not

01:04:16.170 --> 01:04:18.540
immediately or not
in a total way.

01:04:18.540 --> 01:04:23.050
So I feel like that's
a difference, too.

01:04:23.050 --> 01:04:25.680
Yeah, thank you so much.

01:04:25.680 --> 01:04:28.540
Yeah, I guess one thing I
was also thinking about,

01:04:28.540 --> 01:04:31.690
maybe to bring it to a
completely different scale

01:04:31.690 --> 01:04:38.080
and maybe direction
is, in some ways,

01:04:38.080 --> 01:04:44.600
I think these kind of legacies
of plant representation and--

01:04:44.600 --> 01:04:47.650
I think a lot about, I
think, history of photography

01:04:47.650 --> 01:04:49.690
as being deeply entangled
with the history

01:04:49.690 --> 01:04:54.460
of botanical representation
and illustration.

01:04:54.460 --> 01:05:01.270
And in some ways,
I feel that it's

01:05:01.270 --> 01:05:07.030
this pursuit of chlorophyll,
of light, of greenness

01:05:07.030 --> 01:05:11.650
that led to the creation
of a lot of technologies

01:05:11.650 --> 01:05:17.710
of vision, of visualization,
of preservation.

01:05:17.710 --> 01:05:22.345
And that has been
a really central--

01:05:24.850 --> 01:05:28.810
yeah, just a central theme
for some of my practice, and I

01:05:28.810 --> 01:05:31.600
feel it's underlying a
lot of these questions

01:05:31.600 --> 01:05:36.250
of colonial networks
of exchange and what

01:05:36.250 --> 01:05:41.740
was the driving force behind
a lot of these exchanges

01:05:41.740 --> 01:05:43.840
and transportations.

01:05:43.840 --> 01:05:50.716
And I don't want to
forget about, I guess,

01:05:50.716 --> 01:05:54.730
the plants themselves,
the vegetal agency

01:05:54.730 --> 01:05:58.990
and who is actually
cultivating whom?

01:05:58.990 --> 01:06:01.577
Was it humans who were
actually the drivers

01:06:01.577 --> 01:06:02.410
of these exchanges--

01:06:02.410 --> 01:06:02.910
Wow.

01:06:02.910 --> 01:06:11.640
--or did plants
themselves cultivate us?

01:06:11.640 --> 01:06:15.410
It's important to move whose
desires are we looking at.

01:06:15.410 --> 01:06:17.020
And so that is a
kind of question

01:06:17.020 --> 01:06:19.570
that I've been thinking
about for a while, which

01:06:19.570 --> 01:06:21.220
I don't know--

01:06:21.220 --> 01:06:25.120
we're running way
over time, but I just

01:06:25.120 --> 01:06:29.920
wanted to bring that
to the conversation.

01:06:29.920 --> 01:06:33.580
But I think maybe this is
a good time to wrap it up.

01:06:33.580 --> 01:06:34.990
I wanted to thank
everyone again.

01:06:34.990 --> 01:06:38.050
Sarah, thank you so
much for everything.

01:06:38.050 --> 01:06:42.010
Semine and Shireen, I am
so grateful and so inspired

01:06:42.010 --> 01:06:44.560
by both of you, and I
hope that we can continue

01:06:44.560 --> 01:06:46.870
this conversation further.

01:06:46.870 --> 01:06:53.170
And I'm really looking
forward to see what

01:06:53.170 --> 01:06:56.618
else you guys are working on.

01:06:56.618 --> 01:06:58.690
And thank you,
everyone, for being here

01:06:58.690 --> 01:07:02.040
and have a good night.

